13:38:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Stereotypes usually have *some* origin, but "everyone believes it" isn't actually evidence it's accurate or still true. A few things worth pushing back on: **Where the stereotype comes from vs. current reality** - The "nerdy guy" image comes largely from decades where engineering was overwhelmingly male and pop culture ran with a narrow archetype (think 80s/90s media). That doesn't mean it reflects who's actually in the field now. - Women now make up a meaningful chunk of many engineering programs—20-30%+ in mechanical/electrical, and 40%+ in fields like biomedical or environmental. That's not "full of guys," that's a lot of people who aren't fitting your stereotype at all. **Even the "nerdy guys" part oversimplifies things** - Engineering students are actually pretty diverse in personality—athletes, artists, extroverts, musicians, entrepreneurs. The "socially awkward guy obsessed with math" is one type among many, not the default. - Plenty of engineers are perfectly normal, socially adept people who just happen to like building things or solving technical problems. **Why this matters for your sister** If she walks in assuming she'll be surrounded by an awkward monoculture, she might miss that her classmates are a much more varied group than the stereotype suggests—which could actually make the "fitting in" worry evaporate faster than expected. Stereotypes can have a kernel of truth in aggregate data (there probably *is* a higher proportion of certain personality traits in STEM fields), but treating them as the defining truth of "what engineering is" isn't accurate, and could set her up with the wrong expectations going in.
