13:39:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The "generations of people believed it" point is worth taking seriously, but it's actually a different argument than "it's true," and it's worth separating them: **Longevity of a belief isn't the same as accuracy** Lots of things were "common knowledge for generations" that turned out to be wrong or oversimplified: - Women were "naturally bad at math/science" — believed for generations, widely taught, completely wrong - Left-handed people were "sinister" or defective — centuries of belief, no basis - Certain ethnic groups were "naturally" suited to certain labor — generations of "common knowledge," pure social construction Persistence of a belief tells you it was *culturally reinforced*, not that it's *empirically accurate*. Beliefs get passed down for lots of reasons—convenience, reinforcing existing social patterns, incomplete information at the time—not just because they're true. **Why this stereotype specifically became "common knowledge"** Engineering was, for a long time, actually almost entirely male (hence "nerdy guys" becoming shorthand)—so parents/teachers weren't inventing something from nothing. They were describing what they observed at the time. But: - What was true in the 1970s-90s workforce isn't automatically true of a 2024 engineering program - "Common knowledge" often lags behind reality by a generation or more, because stereotypes are sticky even after the underlying demographics shift **So what's actually going on** Your parents and teachers weren't wrong about what they observed in their era. But "this was broadly true when I was young" and "this is still the defining truth of the field now" are different claims. The stereotype had real roots—it's just calcified past the point where it still accurately describes the field your sister's walking into.
