13:39:57 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The argument has some intuitive appeal, but there are real issues with it—the causal claim that work shapes personality conflates selection effects with actual causation, the "nerdy" outcome isn't clearly demonstrated, and many engineering roles actually require substantial teamwork and communication rather than isolated work. Plus, other quantitative fields don't consistently produce people with those same characteristics, which suggests the mechanism doesn't hold up as universally as claimed.
13:39:57 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a more interesting argument than the previous ones—you're proposing a causal mechanism instead of just pointing at tradition. Worth examining seriously. **The selection effect problem** Here's the thing: people who already like math and solo focused work are the ones who choose engineering in the first place. So when you see "engineers doing lots of math/computer work alone," you might be looking at **selection** (people who already had those tendencies chose this path) rather than **causation** (the job made them that way). This matters because if it's selection, then someone who's naturally social/extroverted probably won't choose the field in the first place—but that doesn't mean the field makes people antisocial. It just filters for existing traits, and imperfectly at that. **The actual job doesn't match "hours of math alone"** This is also somewhat outdated as a description of the field: - Most engineering roles involve substantial team collaboration—design reviews, cross-functional meetings, client presentations - A lot of modern engineering work is project management, communication with non-technical stakeholders, writing reports - Coding/analysis work often happens in pairs or teams (code reviews, pair programming), not solo grinding - Even in school, group projects and labs are a huge part of engineering curricula, not just solo problem sets **Other math/computer-heavy jobs don't uniformly produce "nerdy" people** Financial analysts, actuaries, radiologists, data scientists, architects—all involve serious solo technical/quantitative work. Yet we don't have as strong a cultural "nerdy" stereotype for actuaries or radiologists specifically. That suggests the "nerdy" label attached to engineering is doing more cultural/historical work than pure "this type of labor causes this type of personality." **Bottom line** The mechanism you're describing (intense solo technical work shapes personality) has some plausibility in general, but it's more likely explaining *why certain people are drawn to the field* than *why the field turns people into something they weren't*. And the "hours of math and computer work alone" premise is increasingly inaccurate for what the actual day-to-day job looks like now.
