13:40:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a false dilemma—there's a third, much more likely option you're skipping: **Option 3: You've met plenty of non-stereotypical engineers, but didn't notice/count them the same way** This is basic confirmation bias, and it's incredibly well-documented in psychology: - When someone matches the stereotype, it confirms what you already expect, so it sticks in memory ("yep, checks out") - When someone *doesn't* match the stereotype, they don't register as "an engineer defying stereotypes"—they just register as "a normal person," and you may not have consciously filed them under "engineer" in your mental tally at all - You're not doing a rigorous count of every engineer you've met and their personality traits—you're recalling salient, stereotype-confirming examples, which is exactly how stereotypes perpetuate themselves even when reality is more mixed **Also worth noting: your sample isn't random** Who you've personally met depends heavily on your own social circles, hobbies, what contexts you interact with engineers in, etc. If your engineer encounters have been concentrated in, say, certain hobby groups, certain workplaces, or certain online spaces, that's not a representative sample of "engineers" as a whole—it's a sample of "engineers who happen to be in my orbit." **Reframing your question** The real choice isn't "stereotype is true" vs. "everyone I've met is a weird outlier." It's: - "Stereotype reflects a real average tendency, which I've noticed and remembered selectively" (very likely) - vs. "Stereotype is a rigid, universal truth about every individual in the field" (not supported by actual demographic/personality data) Both can be true at once: the trait distribution among engineers probably does skew somewhat toward introversion/technical focus compared to the general population, AND you've likely met plenty of engineers who didn't fit that mold without consciously registering them as counterexamples.
