13:46:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely interesting example because it's a different kind of claim than what we've covered — you're describing actual change over time in one person, which is worth taking on its own terms. **Possible explanations, not mutually exclusive:** 1. **Environment reinforcing/permission-giving** — This is plausible. If he was already somewhat inclined toward technical interests but didn't feel like expressing them fully in a general high school social environment, being around like-minded people can make it feel safe/normal to lean into that identity more openly. Not the environment "turning him into" something new, but giving him license to be more himself. 2. **Selection bias in what you noticed** — You might be seeing him now filtered through "engineering student" framing, so behaviors you'd have shrugged off before (talking about a video game, geeking out on a topic) now get coded as "nerdy" because of the context you know he's in. 3. **General college transition effects, not engineering-specific** — Lots of people get "weirder" or more intensely into niche interests in college generally, regardless of major — new independence, immersion in a subculture, finding "their people" for the first time. This happens in theater programs, gaming communities, fraternities, art school, etc. It's not unique to engineering — it's what happens when someone finds a tribe. 4. **Actual environmental shaping** — I won't rule this out entirely; intense immersion in a subculture can genuinely shift someone's habits, vocabulary, interests over time. Spend years around a specific group and you probably will pick up some of their traits. This does happen. **Bottom line** Probably some mix of #1-3, possibly with a touch of #4. But one cousin's transformation is a single data point — it tells you something happened to him, not that the environment reliably does this to everyone who enters it. Plenty of people go through engineering programs and come out with the same personality they went in with, just with more technical knowledge.
